WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.354 --> 00:00:04.591
Welcome to the third lecture.

2
00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.000
I hope you made good progress with
the assignment from the last session.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.960
Our next step in becoming more
precise about our use of language for

4
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:16.240
use in mathematics is to take a close
look at the meaning of the word implies.

5
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.150
This turns out to be pretty tricky.

6
00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:21.060
Brace yourself for

7
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:26.230
several days of confusion until the idea
sought themselves out in your mind.

8
00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:28.500
Just like learning to ride a bike.

9
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.820
Well, all the actual learning occurs
before you finally get the hang of it.

10
00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.170
So too, most of the benefit
from understanding the way

11
00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:39.620
our language is used in mathematics
comes from trying to figure it out.

12
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:45.190
The benefits in this case is helping
to develop your mathematical thinking

13
00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:47.170
ability, and

14
00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:50.410
extra process of trying to understand
the issue that goes out for you.

15
00:00:51.905 --> 00:00:55.360
Yeah, sure, once you've sorted
this language stuff out,

16
00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:57.150
you'll be able to use
language well actually.

17
00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:03.571
But by that stage, you'll just using it,
the part that helps you with when and

18
00:01:03.571 --> 00:01:06.510
how to think mathematically
is largely over.

19
00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:12.760
We'll need other tasks to develop your
mathematical thinking ability forever.

20
00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:17.000
So as you get into this, bear in mind
that the pay off for struggling with

21
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.060
issues is significant in terms of being
able to think like a mathematician.

22
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:22.800
Here we go.

23
00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:28.150
In mathematics, we frequently encounter
expressions of the form, Phi implies Psi.

24
00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:33.920
Indeed, implication is the means by
which we prove results in mathematics,

25
00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.750
starting with observations or axioms.

26
00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:40.020
So we'd better understand how
the word implies behaves.

27
00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:44.793
In particular, how does the truth or
falsity of a statement Phi implies Psi

28
00:01:44.793 --> 00:01:47.990
depend upon the truth or
falsity of Phi and of Psi?

29
00:01:51.249 --> 00:01:55.910
Well, the obvious answer is to
say that Phi implies Psi if

30
00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:59.710
the truth of Psi follows
from the truth of Phi.

31
00:02:06.427 --> 00:02:07.530
But is that what we want?

32
00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:13.250
Let me give you an example, that phi
of the statement loop 2 is a rational.

33
00:02:17.065 --> 00:02:21.350
And that psi of the statement
is 0 is less than 1.

34
00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:26.111
And let's ask ourselves,

35
00:02:26.111 --> 00:02:33.153
is the statements phi implies psi true?

36
00:02:35.369 --> 00:02:37.030
Well, phi is true.

37
00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:41.960
As I've mentioned once already, we're
going to prove that later in the course.

38
00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.820
And we all know that psi is true,
0 is certainly less than 1.

39
00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:49.170
So we have the truth of this.

40
00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:50.010
We have the truth of that.

41
00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:53.920
Does that mean that phi implies psi?

42
00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:55.420
Obviously not.

43
00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.860
There's no relationship between phi and
psi.

44
00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:01.254
This takes some effort to prove,
as you'll see.

45
00:03:01.254 --> 00:03:02.220
We all know this one.

46
00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:06.360
So yes, this is true and that's true, but

47
00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.550
the truth of this doesn't
follow from the truth of that.

48
00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.590
And now we realize there's
a complexity with implication that

49
00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:16.400
we didn't meet before when we were
dealing with and, with or, and with not.

50
00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:21.050
And the complexity is that implication

51
00:03:26.646 --> 00:03:28.459
Involves causality.

52
00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:42.142
Causality is an issue of great complexity
that philosophers have been discussing for

53
00:03:42.142 --> 00:03:43.010
generations.

54
00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:45.570
Now, we're facing a problem.

55
00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:51.480
It didn't arise before, because when
we're dealing with conjunction and

56
00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:54.090
disjunction, it didn't
matter whether there was

57
00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:56.980
any kind of relationship between
the two conjuncts or the two disjuncts.

58
00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:03.680
For example, let's look at the sentence,
Julius Caesar is dead,

59
00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:09.955
and let's conjoin it with the sentence
1 + 1 = 3, the mathematical sentence.

60
00:04:13.899 --> 00:04:15.485
And let's do the same
thing with disjunction.

61
00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:20.477
Forming a conjunction and

62
00:04:20.477 --> 00:04:24.826
disjunction didn't require any kind
of relationship between these two.

63
00:04:24.826 --> 00:04:26.090
Clearly, they're independent.

64
00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:29.430
One's a statement about
a long dead individual, and

65
00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:30.830
the other one is a mathematical statement.

66
00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:33.688
Incidentally, why do we
got these in front of us?

67
00:04:33.688 --> 00:04:35.962
Let me just give you a quick quiz.

68
00:04:35.962 --> 00:04:39.040
Is the first one true, or is it false?

69
00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:44.170
Is the second one true, or is it false?

70
00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:44.670
What do you think?

71
00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:48.960
Well, remember what the definition was.

72
00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.540
A conjunction is true if
both conjuncts are true.

73
00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:57.580
This one's true but this one's false,
so the conjunction's false.

74
00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:00.450
This one's true.

75
00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:02.030
This one's false.

76
00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:02.620
All that you need for

77
00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:05.860
a disjunction to be true is at least
one of the disjuncts to be true.

78
00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:06.570
So that one's true.

79
00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:12.560
And the fact that there's no
meaningful relationship between the two

80
00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:15.050
conjuncts in this case, or
the two disjuncts in that case,

81
00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:19.060
created no lull in determining
what the truth value was.

82
00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:21.010
It was purely in terms of truth and
falsity.

83
00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:27.890
But it's not sitting with implication,
because implication involves causality.

84
00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:33.400
So let me just express that explicitly.

85
00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:36.820
Implication has a truth part and
a causation part.

86
00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:43.550
What we're going to do
is ignore that part.

87
00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:46.620
We're going to leave that to the
philosophers if you like and we're just

88
00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:52.510
going to focus on the truth part, now that
might sound to be a very rush thing to do.

89
00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:57.000
Throwing away a causation, now,
we can't be left with anything useful, but

90
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.420
it turns out yeah,
it might seem a dangerous thing to do,

91
00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:05.170
to throw away this important causation
implication, but it turns out when we

92
00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:09.250
focus on the truth part we are left with
enough to save our leaves in mathematics.

93
00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.910
So much so
that we're going to give this a name.

94
00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:15.274
We're going to call it the conditional.

95
00:06:20.672 --> 00:06:22.420
Or sometimes, the material conditional.

96
00:06:26.935 --> 00:06:28.564
That's the part we're going to focus on.

97
00:06:32.561 --> 00:06:37.760
So we're going to split implication into
two parts, conditional and causation.

98
00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:41.970
The first part, the conditional,

99
00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:45.690
we're going to define entirely
in terms of truth values.

100
00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:47.452
And the second part we're going
to leave to the philosophers.

101
00:06:51.472 --> 00:06:54.167
The symbol that we use normally for
conditional,

102
00:06:54.167 --> 00:06:57.260
at least the symbol I'm going to use,
is a double arrow.

103
00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:04.932
Lots of textbooks use a single arrow,
but that has many uses in mathematics,

104
00:07:04.932 --> 00:07:08.400
so we certainly are going to use that for
something else later on in the course.

105
00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:12.260
So to avoid confusion, I'm going to stick
to this notation for the conditional.

106
00:07:15.501 --> 00:07:19.320
So I'm going to write conditional
expressions like this.

107
00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:23.037
That's the truth part of phi implies psi.

108
00:07:28.145 --> 00:07:33.127
When we have a conditional,
we call phi the antecedent, and

109
00:07:33.127 --> 00:07:35.480
we call psi the consequent.

110
00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:42.528
And we're going to formally define
the truth of phi conditional psi in

111
00:07:42.528 --> 00:07:45.698
terms of the truth of phi,
and the truth of psi.

112
00:07:52.857 --> 00:07:55.812
Well, you might worry that by
throwing away a causation,

113
00:07:55.812 --> 00:07:59.450
we're going to be left with a notion
that's really of no use whatsoever.

114
00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:01.472
That actually is not the case.

115
00:08:01.472 --> 00:08:06.198
Even though we're throwing away
something of great significance,

116
00:08:06.198 --> 00:08:10.440
hanging on to the truth part
leaves us something very useful.

117
00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:15.614
And the reason is,
whenever we have a genuine implication,

118
00:08:15.614 --> 00:08:20.088
which are actually the only
circumstances in which we're ultimately

119
00:08:20.088 --> 00:08:24.332
going to be interested,
whenever we have a genuine implication,

120
00:08:24.332 --> 00:08:28.550
the truth behavior of
the conditional is the correct one.

121
00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:31.739
It really does capture what
happens with truth and falsity,

122
00:08:31.739 --> 00:08:33.686
when we have a genuine implication.

123
00:08:45.304 --> 00:08:48.555
That probably seems a bit
mysterious at this stage, but

124
00:08:48.555 --> 00:08:53.337
when we start to look at some examples, I
think it should become clear what I mean.

125
00:08:53.337 --> 00:08:58.550
The advantage is that
the conditional is always defined.

126
00:09:03.184 --> 00:09:08.108
For real implication, you've got that
issue of causation, the square root

127
00:09:08.108 --> 00:09:13.030
of 2 and the 0 less than 1 example,
the truth or falsity wasn't the issue,

128
00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:17.770
it was whether there was a relationship
between those two statements.

129
00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:18.920
Now, that's a complicated issue.

130
00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:25.710
But because we're going to
define the conditional purely in

131
00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:30.620
terms of the truth value of the two
constituents, the antecedent and

132
00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:35.580
the consequent, it turns out that
the conditional will always be defined.

133
00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:38.010
When we do have a genuine implication,

134
00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:41.990
the definition of the conditional will
agree with the way implication behaves.

135
00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:45.380
And when we don't have
a genuine implication,

136
00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:49.400
the conditional will still be defined,
and so we can proceed.

137
00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:55.060
Again, this probably seems very mysterious
when I describe it in this way.

138
00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:56.241
But as we develop some examples,

139
00:09:56.241 --> 00:09:58.487
I hope you'll be able to understand
what I'm trying to get at.

140
00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:05.956
Okay, I think we need
to catch our breaths.

141
00:10:05.956 --> 00:10:07.589
Let me do that by giving you a quiz.

142
00:10:12.069 --> 00:10:16.125
The truth of the conditional from
phi to psi is defined in terms of?

143
00:10:20.241 --> 00:10:25.158
1, the truth of phi and psi, or 2,

144
00:10:25.158 --> 00:10:29.780
whether phi causes psi, or both.

145
00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:32.030
Which is it?

146
00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:34.250
It's number 1.

147
00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:40.650
We define the truth of a conditional
in terms of the truth and

148
00:10:40.650 --> 00:10:42.900
falsity of the antecedents and
the consequent.

149
00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:47.490
And because we define the truth of
the conditional in terms of truth and

150
00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:50.340
falsity in that way, it has a truth table.

151
00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:52.850
So let's see if we can
figure out what it is.

152
00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:57.550
Well, the, I'm not giving you,
this is a quiz, by the way,

153
00:10:57.550 --> 00:10:59.190
I'm going to work this one through.

154
00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:03.230
This is tricky, we're not out of
the woods yet by any means, so

155
00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:04.340
I'm going to lead you through this one.

156
00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:06.830
This part we've already looked at.

157
00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:14.460
We define the conditional as
the truth part of implication.

158
00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:19.270
And implication has a property

159
00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:24.200
that a true implication leads to a true
conclusion from a true assumption.

160
00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:29.160
So because we take the conditional
from real implication,

161
00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:31.670
we have to have truths all
the way throughout the top level.

162
00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:35.780
Now the fun begins.

163
00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:39.543
We have to fill in these three values.

164
00:11:39.543 --> 00:11:45.205
And when I say fun, I mean fun.

165
00:11:45.205 --> 00:11:46.979
Let's look at that first
row of the truth table.

166
00:11:50.683 --> 00:11:56.320
Suppose phi is the statement
N is bigger than 7.

167
00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:02.800
And suppose psi is the statement
N squared is bigger than 40.

168
00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:08.601
If phi is true, in other words,
if N is bigger than 7,

169
00:12:08.601 --> 00:12:13.538
then N squared is
certainly bigger than 40.

170
00:12:13.538 --> 00:12:16.642
In fact, it's bigger than 40 now.

171
00:12:16.642 --> 00:12:22.040
So, certainly, in this case,
phi does imply psi.

172
00:12:27.819 --> 00:12:30.623
So that thing is true.

173
00:12:30.623 --> 00:12:36.461
If N is bigger than 7, then N squared is
certainly bigger than 40, so that's true.

174
00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:45.610
We have truth everywhere.

175
00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:47.710
So this is consistent
with the truth table.

176
00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:51.680
Now let's look at a different example.

177
00:12:52.840 --> 00:12:58.537
Let phi be the statement our old
friend Julius Caesar is dead.

178
00:12:58.537 --> 00:13:04.423
And let psi be the statement
pi is bigger than 3.

179
00:13:04.423 --> 00:13:07.243
Phi is true.

180
00:13:07.243 --> 00:13:08.900
Psi is true.

181
00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:12.904
According to the truth table, it follows
that phi conditional psi is true.

182
00:13:17.418 --> 00:13:24.345
In other words, Julius Caesar is dead,
conditional, pi is bigger than 3.

183
00:13:27.370 --> 00:13:32.202
Now, if you read this as Julius Caesar
is dead implies pi bigger than 3,

184
00:13:32.202 --> 00:13:34.640
then you're in a nonsensical situation.

185
00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:39.710
But remember, this isn't implication, this
is just the truth part of implication.

186
00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:43.366
And in terms of the truth part,
there's no problem.

187
00:13:43.366 --> 00:13:49.660
That's true, that's true,
the conditional is true.

188
00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:52.940
In the first example,

189
00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:55.500
there is a meaningful
relationship between phi and psi.

190
00:13:57.130 --> 00:13:59.347
When we know that N is bigger than 7,

191
00:13:59.347 --> 00:14:03.371
then we can conclude that N squared
is certainly bigger than 40.

192
00:14:03.371 --> 00:14:07.912
There's a connection between the two.

193
00:14:07.912 --> 00:14:11.619
And in that case, the behavior of the
conditional is certainly consistent with

194
00:14:11.619 --> 00:14:12.850
what's really going on.

195
00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:16.820
In the second case,
there's no connection between the two.

196
00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:20.447
The conditional is true, but

197
00:14:20.447 --> 00:14:23.623
it's got nothing to do with one
thing following from the other.

198
00:14:27.075 --> 00:14:32.343
The value of doing this is,
even though this has no meaning

199
00:14:32.343 --> 00:14:37.300
in terms of implication,
its truth value is defined.

200
00:14:39.270 --> 00:14:45.380
In both cases,
we have a well-defined truth value.

201
00:14:45.380 --> 00:14:47.660
In the first case,
it's a meaningful truth value.

202
00:14:49.990 --> 00:14:52.050
That does follow from that.

203
00:14:52.050 --> 00:14:56.519
In the second case,
it's purely a defined truth value.

204
00:14:59.326 --> 00:15:01.581
But that's not going to
cause us any problems,

205
00:15:01.581 --> 00:15:05.700
because we're never going to encounter
this kind of thing in mathematics.

206
00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:08.150
We encounter this kind
of thing all the time.

207
00:15:08.150 --> 00:15:10.280
But we're not going to
encounter this kind of thing.

208
00:15:10.280 --> 00:15:12.690
So all we've done is
we've extended a notion

209
00:15:13.730 --> 00:15:16.870
to be defined under all circumstances.

210
00:15:16.870 --> 00:15:18.890
And we've done it in
a way that's consistent

211
00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:23.240
with the behavior we want when
something meaningful is going on.

212
00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:27.970
This is actually quite common in
mathematics to extend the domain of

213
00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:30.570
definition of something so
that it's always defined.

214
00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:35.930
So long as it has the correct behavior,
the correct definition for

215
00:15:35.930 --> 00:15:40.260
the meaningful cases, and
provided we do the definition correctly,

216
00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:41.850
it really doesn't cause any problems.

217
00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:45.090
In fact, it solves a lot of problems and
eliminates a lot of difficulties

218
00:15:46.600 --> 00:15:50.250
if we extend the definition so
that it covers all cases.

219
00:15:50.250 --> 00:15:53.407
Is it just something we do
in mathematics all the time?

220
00:15:53.407 --> 00:15:56.332
May seem strange when you first meet it,
but

221
00:15:56.332 --> 00:15:59.347
it is a part of modern
advanced mathematics.

222
00:15:59.347 --> 00:16:03.032
Incidentally, if you think
this is just playing games,

223
00:16:03.032 --> 00:16:07.717
Let me mention that the computer system
that controls that aircraft that

224
00:16:07.717 --> 00:16:11.018
you'll be flying in next
time depends upon the fact

225
00:16:11.018 --> 00:16:14.500
that expressions like this
are always well defined.

226
00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:18.170
That software control
system doesn't depend upon

227
00:16:19.730 --> 00:16:22.140
knowing whether Julius Caesar is dead or
things like that.

228
00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:24.340
It doesn't depend on those
kind of facts of the world.

229
00:16:24.340 --> 00:16:28.210
Computer systems, by and large,
don't depend upon understanding causation,

230
00:16:28.210 --> 00:16:30.380
which is just as well, because they don't.

231
00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:33.641
What computer systems depend upon is
that things are always accurately and

232
00:16:33.641 --> 00:16:34.580
precisely defined.

233
00:16:37.345 --> 00:16:38.762
And this expression,

234
00:16:38.762 --> 00:16:43.770
phi conditional psi occurs all over
the place in software systems.

235
00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:44.640
So, quite literally,

236
00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:48.270
your life depends upon the fact
that this is always well-defined.

237
00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:50.571
It doesn't depend upon the fact that
the computer doesn't know whether

238
00:16:50.571 --> 00:16:51.520
Julius Caesar is dead or not.

239
00:16:56.818 --> 00:17:00.777
Okay, time to look at the second
row of the truth table.

240
00:17:05.012 --> 00:17:10.580
What goes in here, T or F?

241
00:17:10.580 --> 00:17:12.170
Well we've only got two to choose from.

242
00:17:12.170 --> 00:17:14.080
I guess we could just make a guess but
we're not going to do that,

243
00:17:14.080 --> 00:17:15.760
we're going to figure it out and
get the right answer.

244
00:17:17.070 --> 00:17:21.180
Well, what would happen
if we put a T here.

245
00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:24.430
Let me put it in very lightly,
because I'm sure it's going to be a T.

246
00:17:24.430 --> 00:17:27.120
If this were true,

247
00:17:29.670 --> 00:17:33.220
when we think about it in terms of genuine
implication, because we are trying to

248
00:17:33.220 --> 00:17:36.430
capture the truth behavior with
genuine implication, remember.

249
00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:42.320
So if it was the case that
phi really did imply psi.

250
00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:46.500
If that statement was true when we
interpret it as real implication

251
00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:52.420
then the truth of psi would
follow from the truth of phi.

252
00:17:52.420 --> 00:17:54.260
That's how we began remember.

253
00:17:54.260 --> 00:17:55.660
That's what real implication means,

254
00:17:55.660 --> 00:17:59.820
real implication means the truth of this
would follow from the truth of that.

255
00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:03.420
So if that were a T, and
it was real implication,

256
00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:07.130
then when we have a T here,
we would have to have a T there.

257
00:18:07.130 --> 00:18:10.610
But we don't, we've got an F.

258
00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:16.050
So we can't have a T here,
because if we put a T here the conditional

259
00:18:16.050 --> 00:18:20.960
is contrary, it contradicts real
implication and we're trying to extend

260
00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:24.440
implication to be defined in all
cases where there's no causation.

261
00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:26.810
So this has to be an F.

262
00:18:29.380 --> 00:18:31.610
If we put a T there we're in trouble.

263
00:18:31.610 --> 00:18:33.670
Our notion doesn't agree
with real implication.

264
00:18:34.940 --> 00:18:42.480
In order that the conditional agrees with
real implication, that has to be an F.

265
00:18:43.810 --> 00:18:48.730
It's if a truth, then we would have

266
00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:53.610
a true antecedent and a false
consequence from a true implication.

267
00:18:55.230 --> 00:18:57.920
So we've argued backwards to
conclude that this has to be F.

268
00:18:59.710 --> 00:19:02.270
Let me write that down just to make
sure everyone's following what I'm

269
00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:02.880
trying to say.

270
00:19:04.570 --> 00:19:08.310
If there were a genuine
implication phi implies psi and

271
00:19:08.310 --> 00:19:12.750
if that implication were true,
then psi would have to be true.

272
00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:13.647
If phi were true.

273
00:19:17.618 --> 00:19:23.380
So we cannot have phi true and
psi false, if phi implies psi is true.

274
00:19:26.418 --> 00:19:32.956
That means,
that in the case where phi is true.

275
00:19:32.956 --> 00:19:36.680
And psi is false,
we have to have a false here.

276
00:19:39.848 --> 00:19:40.709
Let me write that down.

277
00:19:47.501 --> 00:19:48.505
Are you confused?

278
00:19:49.805 --> 00:19:50.510
This is tricky.

279
00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:54.345
There's no getting around the fact
that sorting out implications and

280
00:19:54.345 --> 00:19:58.075
then extracting conditional
form implications is tricky.

281
00:19:59.170 --> 00:20:03.270
You're probably going to have to
replay the videos several times and

282
00:20:03.270 --> 00:20:07.010
look at what's been written and
think about it in order to sort this out.

283
00:20:08.900 --> 00:20:12.940
I remember when I first met this, it took
me quite a while to get on top of it.

284
00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:15.380
It really isn't an easy thing to do.

285
00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:17.435
So, you certainly have my sympathy
that you're going to have to

286
00:20:17.435 --> 00:20:19.300
struggle with this thing.

287
00:20:19.300 --> 00:20:22.135
It's probably one of the hardest
things in the course,

288
00:20:22.135 --> 00:20:26.074
to really come to terms with the way we
define the meaning of the conditional.

289
00:20:29.389 --> 00:20:31.340
Okay, final straight.

290
00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:33.930
Let's see if we can fill in the last
two entries in the truth table for

291
00:20:33.930 --> 00:20:34.980
the conditional.

292
00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:37.171
When we've done that, we'll all
go off in search of some aspirin.

293
00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:48.020
Now if you're like me, you have no
intuitions as to what to put here.

294
00:20:48.020 --> 00:20:50.920
And the reason you have no
intuition is that even though

295
00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:53.520
you're used to dealing with implication

296
00:20:53.520 --> 00:20:58.070
you've never dealt with an implication
where the antecedent was false.

297
00:20:58.070 --> 00:21:03.400
You're only ever interested in drawing
conclusions from true assumptions.

298
00:21:04.440 --> 00:21:06.799
So you never dealt with
a true one of these guys.

299
00:21:08.230 --> 00:21:09.420
When this guy was false.

300
00:21:11.710 --> 00:21:12.840
You don't have any intuitions.

301
00:21:15.140 --> 00:21:18.684
You do however have
intuitions about this guy.

302
00:21:25.198 --> 00:21:32.470
And the reason that's going to help us out
is that negation, swaps around F and T.

303
00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:42.640
So corresponding to the Fs here, when
we look at this guy we'll have truths.

304
00:21:42.640 --> 00:21:44.800
We'll have Ts for this.

305
00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:48.259
So you are used to having
to deal with this.

306
00:21:50.080 --> 00:21:50.930
When this is true,

307
00:21:50.930 --> 00:21:55.180
and that will be equivalent to
dealing with this when that's false.

308
00:21:55.180 --> 00:21:56.990
Because negation swaps truth and falsity.

309
00:21:58.710 --> 00:22:03.520
So the trick, or at least the idea by
which we're going to figure out what goes

310
00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:08.130
here, is to stop looking
at implication and

311
00:22:08.130 --> 00:22:13.270
look at not implication.

312
00:22:13.270 --> 00:22:19.365
Phi does not imply psi if
even though phi is true,

313
00:22:19.365 --> 00:22:22.940
psi is nevertheless false.

314
00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:30.875
Just think about that for
a minute, phi does not imply psi

315
00:22:30.875 --> 00:22:35.650
if even though phi is true
psi is nevertheless false.

316
00:22:38.050 --> 00:22:41.860
That's how you know that this guy holds.

317
00:22:41.860 --> 00:22:47.449
You know that phi doesn't imply psi
if you can check that phi is true but

318
00:22:47.449 --> 00:22:49.735
psi is nevertheless false.

319
00:22:53.200 --> 00:22:57.470
That's the only circumstance under which
you can conclude this thing is true.

320
00:22:58.850 --> 00:23:02.560
In all other circumstances,
this guy will be false.

321
00:23:02.560 --> 00:23:03.490
So let me write that down.

322
00:23:09.204 --> 00:23:10.578
Okay?

323
00:23:10.578 --> 00:23:16.650
This guy is true if you have phi true and
psi false.

324
00:23:18.340 --> 00:23:23.750
In all other circumstances,
this guy will be false.

325
00:23:25.600 --> 00:23:27.020
You still with me?

326
00:23:27.020 --> 00:23:29.390
Because we're only one line
away from the conclusion now.

327
00:23:31.260 --> 00:23:34.230
Let me just, Rewrite that as follows.

328
00:23:40.643 --> 00:23:43.984
Because negation swaps F and T,

329
00:23:43.984 --> 00:23:48.702
this guy will be false,
and this guy is true.

330
00:23:52.923 --> 00:24:00.288
So if we have T and F, which we have here.

331
00:24:00.288 --> 00:24:04.860
Then negation is false,
which means this guy is true.

332
00:24:06.360 --> 00:24:12.460
In all other circumstances,
this guy is true, so those are true.

333
00:24:14.730 --> 00:24:15.230
And we're done.

334
00:24:16.860 --> 00:24:19.170
Phew, that was difficult.

335
00:24:19.170 --> 00:24:23.410
In fact, in my mind, this is the most
difficult part of the entire course.

336
00:24:23.410 --> 00:24:24.690
This is not easy stuff,

337
00:24:24.690 --> 00:24:27.980
it's going to take you some time
before you sort it out in your mind.

338
00:24:27.980 --> 00:24:30.010
You're going to have to
keep working at this.

339
00:24:30.010 --> 00:24:32.990
Look through the lecture several times and
think about it.

340
00:24:32.990 --> 00:24:35.940
It will take effort, but you'll get there.

341
00:24:35.940 --> 00:24:37.850
We all get there in the end,
but it's just not easy.

342
00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:41.560
In the meantime, you've got this far.

343
00:24:42.700 --> 00:24:45.895
We should start thinking about
opening a bottle of champagne.

344
00:24:48.527 --> 00:24:51.954
But before you crack open the bottle, let
me leave you with one more little quiz.

345
00:24:54.842 --> 00:24:56.150
Which of the following are true?

346
00:24:58.740 --> 00:25:04.974
Phi conditional psi is true whenever,

347
00:25:07.526 --> 00:25:10.246
(1) Phi and psi are both true.

348
00:25:10.246 --> 00:25:15.196
(2) Phi is false and psi is true.

349
00:25:15.196 --> 00:25:18.116
(3) Phi and psi are both false.

350
00:25:22.032 --> 00:25:28.860
And (4), phi is true and psi is false.

351
00:25:32.010 --> 00:25:33.740
And I want you to check all that are true.

352
00:25:35.470 --> 00:25:40.850
Well, which of these four, Are the case?

353
00:25:41.930 --> 00:25:44.640
Which of these four conditions
tell you when phi, psi is true?

354
00:25:45.920 --> 00:25:49.160
The answer is 1, 2, and 3.

355
00:25:51.734 --> 00:25:55.679
Phi conditional psi is true
either when 1 is true, or

356
00:25:55.679 --> 00:25:59.040
when 2 is true, or when 3 is true.

357
00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:03.630
The only time when phi conditional
psi is false is when 4 holds.

358
00:26:04.640 --> 00:26:10.550
So the correct answer is 1,
2, and 3, and 4 is not.

359
00:26:10.550 --> 00:26:12.271
Okay, now you can open that champagne.

360
00:26:15.395 --> 00:26:16.680
Let me sum up what we've done.

361
00:26:17.930 --> 00:26:20.520
We've defined a notion, the conditional,

362
00:26:20.520 --> 00:26:22.590
that captures only part
of what implies means.

363
00:26:23.640 --> 00:26:25.320
To avoid difficulties,

364
00:26:25.320 --> 00:26:29.400
we base our definition solely on
the notion of truth and falsity.

365
00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:32.590
Our definition agrees with our
intuition concerning implication in

366
00:26:32.590 --> 00:26:34.490
all meaningful cases.

367
00:26:34.490 --> 00:26:37.090
The definition for a true antecedent

368
00:26:37.090 --> 00:26:40.740
is based on an analysis of the truth
values of genuine implication.

369
00:26:41.920 --> 00:26:46.820
The definition for our false antecedents
is based on a truth value analysis

370
00:26:46.820 --> 00:26:48.580
of the notion does not imply.

371
00:26:50.270 --> 00:26:54.850
In defining the conditional the way we do,
we do not end up with a notion that

372
00:26:54.850 --> 00:26:56.970
contradicts a notion of
genuine implication.

373
00:26:58.270 --> 00:27:02.050
Rather, we obtain a notion that
extends genuine implication

374
00:27:02.050 --> 00:27:06.070
to cover those cases where the claim
of implication is irrelevant,

375
00:27:06.070 --> 00:27:10.060
because the antecedent is false or
meaningless

376
00:27:10.060 --> 00:27:13.900
when there's no real connection between
the antecedent and the consequence.

377
00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:19.040
In the meaningful case where there is
a relationship between phi and psi, and

378
00:27:19.040 --> 00:27:22.380
in addition, where phi is true, namely,

379
00:27:22.380 --> 00:27:27.130
the cases covered by the first two rows
of the truth table, the truth value of

380
00:27:27.130 --> 00:27:30.940
the conditional will be the same as
the truth value of the actual implication.

381
00:27:32.770 --> 00:27:36.043
Remember, it's the fact that
the conditional always has

382
00:27:36.043 --> 00:27:40.721
a well-defined truth value that makes this
notion important in mathematics since

383
00:27:40.721 --> 00:27:41.723
in mathematics,

384
00:27:41.723 --> 00:27:46.435
we can't afford to have statements with
undefined truth values floating around.

385
00:27:48.700 --> 00:27:50.590
Let's finish this lecture
with a short quiz.

386
00:27:51.840 --> 00:27:54.900
When you've done that,
you should complete assignment three.

387
00:27:57.320 --> 00:28:01.440
I've kept assignment three fairly
short since I expect you'll need most

388
00:28:01.440 --> 00:28:05.100
of your time simply understanding
our analysis of implication and

389
00:28:05.100 --> 00:28:06.350
the definition of the conditional.

390
00:28:07.780 --> 00:28:10.040
Remember to discuss these
assignments with other students.

391
00:28:11.220 --> 00:28:12.840
Don't struggle for too long on your own.

392
00:28:13.940 --> 00:28:17.700
On this kind of material, it's much
more effective to work with others.

393
00:28:18.720 --> 00:28:19.220
Here's the quiz.

394
00:28:21.475 --> 00:28:24.310
Well, the answer to the first
one is that it's true.

395
00:28:25.720 --> 00:28:30.198
The antecedent is true, and
the consequence is true, so

396
00:28:30.198 --> 00:28:32.262
the conditional is true.

397
00:28:35.751 --> 00:28:37.880
In fact,
there's a deeper result going on here.

398
00:28:39.090 --> 00:28:42.840
Providing you take a positive number,
instead of pi,

399
00:28:42.840 --> 00:28:47.672
any positive number, then if the square
of that positive number is bigger than 2,

400
00:28:47.672 --> 00:28:52.045
that number must be bigger than 1.2.

401
00:28:55.077 --> 00:28:59.090
Because the square root of 2 is 1.4,
etc, etc, etc.

402
00:29:00.390 --> 00:29:06.316
So for positive numbers, it doesn't
have to be pi, it can be anything,

403
00:29:06.316 --> 00:29:13.135
any positive number whose square is bigger
than 2, it must be bigger than 1.2.

404
00:29:13.135 --> 00:29:16.490
And that would be a case of genuine
causation, genuine implication.

405
00:29:18.431 --> 00:29:21.825
But in terms of the conditional,
it's enough that the antecedent is true,

406
00:29:21.825 --> 00:29:23.210
then the consequence is true.

407
00:29:25.697 --> 00:29:28.350
For this one, it's also true.

408
00:29:29.870 --> 00:29:31.360
Now the consequence is false.

409
00:29:32.360 --> 00:29:34.165
Pi certainly does not equal 3.

410
00:29:37.583 --> 00:29:40.200
But the antecedent is false.

411
00:29:41.200 --> 00:29:45.200
Add if you have a false antecedent,
the conditional is always true.

412
00:29:45.200 --> 00:29:47.537
Pi squared is most
certainly not less than 0.

413
00:29:47.537 --> 00:29:51.760
So, you've got false, false,
that makes the conditional true.

414
00:29:54.344 --> 00:29:59.850
Number three, That one's false.

415
00:30:03.060 --> 00:30:07.090
The antecedent is true, and
the consequence is false.

416
00:30:07.090 --> 00:30:11.220
And you cannot obtain a false
conclusion from a true assumption.

417
00:30:13.790 --> 00:30:14.570
What about the next one?

418
00:30:16.630 --> 00:30:17.200
Well, that one's true.

419
00:30:20.120 --> 00:30:23.140
The antecedent is true, and
the consequence is true.

420
00:30:25.250 --> 00:30:25.900
What about this one?

421
00:30:28.200 --> 00:30:29.718
Do triangles have four sides?

422
00:30:29.718 --> 00:30:31.419
No.

423
00:30:31.419 --> 00:30:32.664
Do squares have five sides?

424
00:30:32.664 --> 00:30:34.359
No.

425
00:30:37.064 --> 00:30:42.710
But anything with a false
antecedent is true, so that's true.

426
00:30:43.920 --> 00:30:47.921
You've got false, conditional,
false, and that's always true.

427
00:30:47.921 --> 00:30:48.720
What about this one?

428
00:30:50.050 --> 00:30:51.920
Well, we don't know when
Euclid's birthday was.

429
00:30:51.920 --> 00:30:53.950
At least,
I don't know when Euclid's birthday was.

430
00:30:53.950 --> 00:30:55.303
I suspect you don't either.

431
00:30:57.596 --> 00:31:01.100
Rectangles certainly have four sides,
however.

432
00:31:02.740 --> 00:31:06.470
Either this is true, or it's false.

433
00:31:07.470 --> 00:31:14.277
Either way, since the consequence is true,
the thing is true.

434
00:31:17.341 --> 00:31:22.351
We've going to run down
using the two consequence.

435
00:31:22.351 --> 00:31:27.083
So either we have true, true,
in which case, it's true, or

436
00:31:27.083 --> 00:31:31.009
we have false, true,
in which case, it's true.

437
00:31:31.009 --> 00:31:34.790
Okay, how did you get on with that?